2.1 Mandatory Public Attribution (Masthead/Display)
(a) If You (as defined in MPL 2.0) deploy, host, provide, or otherwise make the Software (or any Modifications or Larger Works that include any files of the Software) publicly accessible to end users (a “Public Instance”), You must display clear attribution to the original author:

    “Remco Dijkman”

(b) Placement. The attribution must appear prominently on the website’s masthead (top navigation/header area) or footer on all public-facing pages of the Public Instance. It must be plainly visible without user interaction (e.g., without hovering, expanding, or scrolling beyond standard viewport for the page’s main content).

(c) Presentation. The attribution must be in a font size and color that is reasonably readable and not obscured or diminished by surrounding elements. “Prominently” means that an ordinary user can readily see the attribution within the masthead or footer as rendered on common desktop and mobile browsers.

(d) Allowed Variations. You may localize the text (language), and may style the attribution to match the site’s design, provided it remains clear, visible, and not misleading. You may include additional credits (e.g., Your company name) provided the above author attribution remains present.

(e) Non-Web Contexts. If the Software is used without a web UI (e.g., solely via an API, CLI, or embedded service), the attribution must appear in publicly accessible documentation, API landing pages, or a default “About” endpoint/command output that is reasonably reachable by users of the Public Instance.

(f) Configuration. You may provide configuration to place the attribution in the masthead or footer, but You may not remove or suppress it for Public Instances.

(g) Trademark. This attribution does not grant any rights in the name “Remco Dijkman” as a trademark. Use of any trademarks is subject to applicable trademark law.

2.2 Network Use Source Availability (SaaS Clause)
(a) If You operate a Public Instance (as defined above), and such Public Instance uses any Modifications (as defined in MPL 2.0) to the Software, then You must make the Source Code Form of those Modifications reasonably and publicly available—at no charge—to all users of the Public Instance, under the MPL 2.0 plus these Additional Terms.

(b) Reasonable Availability. “Reasonably and publicly available” means providing a stable, publicly accessible URL that (i) links to a repository or downloadable archive containing the complete Source Code Form of the Modifications You deploy, and (ii) includes build and deployment instructions sufficient for a technically competent user to compile and run the Modified files in a development environment.

(c) Scope. This clause applies only to Modifications to files that are part of the Software or files that are derivative of such files. It does not require You to open source Your entire Larger Work or proprietary components that merely interface with the Software without creating Modifications to its files.

(d) Timing. You must update the publicly accessible source within a reasonable time after deploying the Modifications (e.g., within 30 days of first public availability of the Public Instance with those Modifications).

(e) License Continuity. Modifications made available under this clause must be licensed under the MPL 2.0 and these Additional Terms. You may also dual-license Modifications under additional terms provided those terms are not incompatible with MPL 2.0 and these Additional Terms.

